  [1mThe Long Way Round[0m

  The tug [3mHester[0m was forty-two feet long
  and painted the red of a fire engine
  left out in the rain for a few
  decades. She had a blunt nose, a fat
  rope fender around her bow like a
  mustache, and an engine that sounded,
  at full throttle, like someone
  dragging a piano down a flight of
  stairs. Walt Duquesne had skippered
  her for twenty-six years. In that time
  she had pushed oil barges,
  freighters, a drydock, a sunken
  dredge, and once, for reasons nobody
  ever explained to him, a floating
  billboard for a mattress company.

  On the morning in question, the job
  sheet said only: [3mTow BLUEBELL, Pier 9
[0m  [3mto Harrow Breakers. Depart 0700.[0m

  Walt read it twice in the harbor
  office, then folded it into his shirt
  pocket and went out to the pier
  without saying anything to anyone.

  The [3mBluebell[0m had been the island ferry
  for sixty-one years. She was white
  and wooden and absurdly ornate, with a
  brass bell on her foredeck and a
  little upper saloon where the windows
  were etched with lilies. Walt had

